 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The computation of layer indices and depths is $O(1)$ per voxel/column using logarithms and exponentiation. Precomputing logarithms can optimize this further [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** The partitioning itself requires minimal memory (a few constants). However, it enables more efficient voxelization by reducing the number of voxels needed for distant objects, potentially lowering memory usage for the froxel grid compared to linear partitioning at equivalent angular resolution [S1].
-- **Performance:** On a GTX 970, pure OpenGL-based rendering using this method was found to be slower than SIMT-based splatting for certain particle sorting scenarios, but the method itself is efficient for voxelization [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Ensures continuous appearance in final renders by maintaining constant angular resolution; reduces depth precision requirements for distant objects.
-- **Limitations:** Requires perspective projection; non-linear spacing may complicate uniform sampling if not handled correctly.
